<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James I of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1208–<a href="/years/1276">1276</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as James the Conqueror</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James I, called the Conqueror, was king of Aragon and count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> from 1213 to 1276. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> in 1229 and the Muslim kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> in 1238, creating new realms with their own laws within the Crown of Aragon. In the Treaty of Corbeil of 1258 he gave up most claims north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. His chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llibre-dels-fets">Book of Deeds</a>, is the first royal autobiography in Catalan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-i-of-aragon.jpg" alt="James I of Aragon" width="162" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vidal de Canellas y cancillería real del rey de Aragón.; derivative work by Escarlati · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jaime_I_de_Arag%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James was born at Montpellier on 2 February 1208, the only son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ii-of-aragon">Peter II of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-montpellier">Marie of Montpellier</a>. His father tried to placate the crusaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> by arranging a marriage between the two-year-old James and Amicie, daughter of Simon IV de Montfort, and in 1211 entrusted the boy to Montfort's care. Peter was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a> on 12 September 1213, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iii">Pope Innocent III</a> insisted that Montfort give James up.</p><p>James was raised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monzon">Monzón</a> by Guillem de Montredó, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> in Aragon and Provence. In 1217 the Templars and some loyal nobles brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. In 1221 he married Eleanor, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and rebellions by nobles filled the next six years until the Peace of Alcalá of 31 March 1227.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After a scheme to inherit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> came to nothing, James turned south and east. On 5 September 1229 a fleet of 155 ships carrying 1,500 horsemen and 15,000 soldiers sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, which fell on 31 December 1229; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a> followed in 1232 and Ibiza in 1235. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> capitulated on 28 September 1238. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-almizra">Treaty of Almizra</a> of 26 March 1244 James and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a> fixed the border of the new Kingdom of Valencia.</p><p>In May 1258 the Treaty of Corbeil ended his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> renounced claims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over Catalonia, and James gave up his rights in Provence. Catalonia's ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> faded as a result. In 1269 he assembled a fleet for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, but a storm drove him to Aigues-Mortes and he abandoned the voyage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>James compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consulate-of-the-sea">Llibre del Consolat de Mar</a>, which governed maritime trade, and sponsored the University of Montpellier. In 1263 he presided over the Barcelona Disputation between the rabbi Nahmanides and the convert Pablo Christiani. He wrote or dictated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/llibre-dels-fets">Llibre dels fets</a>, the first autobiography by a Christian king and an early work of Catalan prose. At the urging of his confessor Ramon de Penyafort he brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> into his realm in 1233.</p><p>In his will he divided his lands between his sons by Yolanda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>: Peter received the mainland kingdoms and James the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, a division that led to conflict between the brothers. James died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> on 27 July 1276.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James I of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>James I</b> the Conqueror left an account of his own reign, the Llibre dels fets, one of the earliest autobiographies by a medieval Christian king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Catalan nobles and towns contributed to <b>James I</b>’s conquest of Majorca in 1229, and the royal shipyards at Barcelona, the Drassanes, built the galleys that made the Crown a leading naval power.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>James I of Aragon</b> signed the Treaty of Almizra with the Castilian heir Alfonso, fixing the frontier between their conquests in al-Andalus.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-26">26 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Louis IX of France and King <b>James I of Aragon</b> signed the Treaty of Corbeil.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-11">11 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating the Moors of the taifa of Balansiya, King <b>James I of Aragon</b> entered the city of Valencia, an event regarded as the dawn of the Kingdom of Valencia, a component realm of the Crown of Aragon.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>James I of Aragon</b>'s army fought the Almohad troops holding Mallorca at Portopí, between Santa Ponsa and the City of Majorca, in the second major battle of his campaign to conquer the island.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-12">12 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montpellier, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.61,3.88&amp;year=1252" aria-label="Montpellier on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4643 618 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James I of Aragon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5093 888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5093 888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.61,3.88&amp;year=1252">What was here in 1252? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
